"""Chinese-romanisation passages (xianxia / wuxia register). Original prose."""

PASSAGES = [
("zh01", """Rain fell on [[Qing Mao Mountain|l]] for three days. [[Fang Yuan|p]] wiped his blade and watched the [[Heavenly Sword Sect|o]] disciples file past. Sect elders did not look at him. The [[clan head|t|sh]] of the [[Fang clan|o]] had sent him here with one rule: never speak of the old quarrel. At the gate, [[Elder Mo|p|tit]] stopped him. “Hunter or disciple?” the old man asked. “Neither,” said [[Fang Yuan|p]]. “I am only a guest.” The elder grunted, and the heavy doors opened onto a courtyard where Heaven's light fell through cloud."""),
("zh02", """[[Bai|p|wl]] was not the kind of man who rushed. He poured tea for the visitors from [[Cold Moon Valley|l]] and waited. “[[Brother Bai|p|tit]],” said the stranger, “your master is called [[Bai Ning Bing|p]] in the south, is he not?” Nobody answered. Later the same man was spoken of as [[Bai Ningbing|p]] by the merchants, as [[the Frost Lord|t|ali]] by the guards, and simply as [[the Venerable|t|ali]] by his students. They all meant one old man, who sat unmoving beside the jade cup called [[Spring Autumn Cicada|i]]."""),
("zh03", """[[Xiao Yan|p]] knelt before the stone tablet. In the older books his name was spelled [[Hsiao Yen|p]], but he had stopped caring about spellings long ago. “[[Xiao Yan-ge|p|tit]],” called a girl from the courtyard, and [[Ling|p|wl]] ran up with a basket of pills. “[[Ling|p|wl]], you should be resting,” he said. “The [[Alchemist Guild|o]] will want its debt paid by autumn,” she answered. Sect rules were strict that year. The [[sect master|t]] of [[Jade Cloud Peak|l]] had forbidden every outside pill."""),
("zh04", """Two clans shared the river valley, and each had a [[clan head|t|sh]]. The [[clan head|t|sh]] of the [[Lu clan|o]] was a patient man. The [[clan head|t|sh]] of the [[Shen clan|o]] was not. When [[Lu Chen|p]] arrived, [[Shen Hu|p]] slammed the table. “Your [[clan head|t|sh]] insults us!” “Mine?” said [[Lu Chen|p]]. “Ask yours about the stolen grain.” Neither of them noticed [[Sung|p|wl]] slipping out through the back door with the ledger. Silence followed, and then a distant bell from [[Nine Dragon Temple|l]] marked the hour."""),
("zh05", """Heaven punishes the arrogant, the saying goes, but [[Wei Ming|p]] had never seen it happen. He climbed the last step and found [[Ancestor Hong|p|tit]] sitting beneath a pine, eating a plain steamed bun. “Sit,” said [[Ancestor Hong|p|tit]]. “You carry the [[Black Tortoise Seal|i]]. Did [[Mister Du|p|tit]] give it to you?” “He did.” The old man laughed. “Then [[Wei Ming|p]], you are late. The [[Dao Society|o]] meets at dusk, and Chapter Nine of its rules is very clear.”"""),
("zh06", """Grace was a word the village used for harvest, but here it was the name of a girl. [[Grace|p|wl]] Tan carried water up the ridge to [[Wolf Fang Camp|l]]. Behind her walked [[Tan Jie|p]], her elder sister, whom the soldiers called [[Second Miss Tan|p|ali]]. “[[Tan-jie|p|tit]],” [[Grace|p|wl]] said, “is it true the [[Iron Banner Army|o]] will march at dawn?” “Ask the general.” At night the two sisters shared a cold meal. The commander, known only as [[the General|t|ali]], never learned that either of them could read maps."""),
("zh07", """The auction hall of [[Ten Thousand Treasures Pavilion|o]] glittered. A man in grey robes, whom everyone called [[Hunter Li|p|tit]], raised one finger. “Three hundred spirit stones for the [[Nine-Turn Reincarnation Pill|i]].” The auctioneer, [[Madam Yu|p|tit]], smiled thinly. “Sold.” Outside, [[Hunter|p|wl]], as the northern merchants shortened it, stood in the rain beside his disciple [[Zhao Rui|p]]. Nobody guessed that the master of [[Floating Lantern City|l]] had once bowed to him."""),
("zh08", """Dawn at [[Heavenly Demon Sect|o]]. The great hall was empty except for a single figure: the [[Heavenly Demon|t|ali]], also written in older records as [[Tian Mo|p]], also known to his servants as [[Master|t|ali]]. “Hope you slept well,” he told the kneeling steward, [[Hope|p|wl]] by courtesy, since his real name was [[Xi Wang|p]]. “[[Xi Wang|p]], send word to the [[Righteous Alliance|o]]. Tell them the sword has returned.” The sword in question was named [[Frost Sorrow|i]], and it hummed faintly in its sheath."""),
("zh09", """Sect rules forbid fighting on the steps, so [[Zhou Mu|p]] only watched. Disciples of the [[Azure Cloud Sect|o]] practised below. A thin boy, [[Joy|p|wl]] by nickname and [[Yue Le|p]] by birth, dropped his wooden sword. “Careless,” said [[Senior Brother Ji|p|tit]]. “[[Brother Ji|p|tit]] is right,” echoed [[Zhou Mu|p]]. Then the [[sect leader|t]] descended from [[Lotus Peak|l]], and everyone bowed. The Sect did not forgive laziness. “Whoever breaks the Sect's rules,” he said, “answers to me.” Nobody spoke."""),
("zh10", """[[Sima Lan|p]] wrote his own name three ways on the wall: [[Sima Lan|p]], [[Ssu-ma Lan|p]], and [[Lan Sima|p]], as the foreigners said it. Then he rubbed all three out. “[[Master Sima|p|tit]],” a servant whispered, “the envoy from [[Vermilion Bird Court|o]] waits.” “Let the envoy wait.” In the corridor stood [[Wang Er|p]], bound by hemp rope. “[[Wang Er|p]] was captured at [[Broken Bridge|l]],” said the guard. “Good,” said [[Sima Lan|p]]. “Then the [[Jade Emperor's Decree|i]] is safe.” Perhaps."""),
("zh11", """Will was the one thing the mountain could not take from [[Will Chen|p|wl]]. He climbed anyway. His teacher, [[Teacher Gu|p|tit]], called him [[Chen Wei|p]] when angry and [[Little Will|p|ali]] when pleased, and both names meant the same boy. At the summit stood the [[Ice Soul Pagoda|l]]. Inside waited the [[Pagoda Keeper|t]], who asked only one question: “What do you want?” “To return the [[Moon Pearl|i]],” said [[Chen Wei|p]]. The keeper smiled. Past that smile, the wind of [[Mount Xuan|l]] howled."""),
("zh12", """In the south, [[Madam Ouyang|p|tit]] ruled the river trade. In the north, another [[Madam Ouyang|p|tit]] ruled the salt trade, and they were not related. Merchants joked about it. “Which [[Madam|t|sh]]?” they asked. Only [[Ouyang Xue|p]], the younger one, found it funny. She sat among her fans at [[Peony Pavilion|l]], reading a letter from [[Ouyang Shan|p]]. “[[Ouyang Shan|p]] wants the [[Tide Ledger|i]],” she said. “Then he can come for it.” The rain over [[Wuxi|l]] never stopped that month."""),
("zh13", """Beauty was a trap, and [[Mei|p|wl]] knew it. At the [[Hundred Flowers Sect|o]] she was called [[Junior Sister Mei|p|tit]]; in the capital, [[Lady Mei|p|tit]]; in the old stories, [[the Plum Blossom Swordswoman|t|ali]]. All three were one woman. “[[Mei-jie|p|tit]]!” cried a young disciple, “the [[sect master|t]] wants you at [[Plum Hall|l]].” She tied her hair and took up the sword [[Winter Whisper|i]]. Nobody on the road recognised her, which was exactly how she wanted it."""),
("zh14", """The two brothers walked beneath [[Heaven's Gate|l]], a pass in the cliffs. [[Gu Yan|p]] was the elder, and the villagers said [[Gu Yan-ge|p|tit]] with respect. [[Gu Bo|p]] was the younger. “Eldest,” [[Gu Bo|p]] teased, “when will the [[Longevity Sect|o]] let us in?” “When the [[Patriarch|t]] dies,” said [[Gu Yan|p]]. “Hush. Heaven hears.” Neither brother carried more than a rice bag and a cracked flute, the [[Bone Flute|i]], which had once belonged to their mother. Stars rose over [[Qilian Pass|l]]."""),
("zh15", """Nothing in the sutra mentioned [[Cang Ming|p]]. Still, the monks of [[Silent Bell Temple|o]] spoke of him in whispers: [[Cang Ming|p]], [[Tsang Ming|p]], the [[Blind Monk|t|ali]]. Three days earlier, [[Abbot Kong|p|tit]] had died; now the abbot's seat stood empty. “Who will lead?” asked [[Novice Pei|p|tit]]. Nobody answered. The wind carried incense across the courtyard of [[Lingyin|l]], and the great bell, [[Thunder Mother|i]], rang once by itself. Chapter Seven of the monastery rules says: a bell that rings unprompted is an omen."""),
]
